      keyPoints: [
        "*hay* = there is / there are — one form for singular and plural.",
        "*hay* + *un/una/unos/unas*, a number, *muchos…* or no article.",
        "*no hay* + noun, normally without an article: *No hay sofá.*",
        "*está / están* = is / are (located), with *el/la/los/las/mi/tu*.",
        "*¿Qué hay…?* → *Hay…*; *¿Dónde está…?* → *Está…*",
        "Location uses *estar*, never *ser*.",
      ],
      whyItWorks:
        "Think about what the listener already knows. *Hay* introduces something brand new to the conversation, so it pairs with the 'new information' words: *un, una*, numbers. Once it has been introduced, it's a specific, known thing — *the* lamp, *my* books — so you use *el/la* and say where it *está*. English works the same way: 'There's a lamp in my room. The lamp is on the desk.'\n\n*Hay* never changes because it has no subject to agree with — it comes from the verb *haber* and simply means 'there exists'. *Está*, on the other hand, agrees with its subject, so a plural subject needs *están*.",
      strategies: ["Look for the pattern", "Check the subject", "Use context", "Eliminate options"],
      thinkDeeper:
        "A learner writes: *En mi dormitorio está una cama. Hay la cama al lado de la ventana.* Both sentences go wrong for the same underlying reason. Explain the reason in one sentence, then fix both. Bonus: why does a Spanish speaker ask *¿Hay un bolígrafo en tu escritorio?* and not *¿Está un bolígrafo en tu escritorio?*",
